{
  "name": "astral-veil-ui-dark",
  "description": "Provides an expert frontend/UI/UX role-prompt plus a full Astral Veil design system - a premium, editorial dark-SaaS aesthetic built on serif-primary Playfair Display headlines, a signature 1px gradient-veil card border that fades from a lit top edge to nothing, quiet low-opacity purple/blue ambient glows, live-UI-mockup feature illustrations (progress bars, file tiles, chat bubbles) instead of icons, floating mini-dashboard notification cards, and a rare inline white-highlighter-block emphasis device - for integrating this calm, confident, literary-meets-technical style into ANY existing codebase or product, universal across website and software types (marketing sites, SaaS dashboards, e-commerce, internal tools, mobile apps, developer tools, portfolios, etc.). Outputs JSON/YAML theme token definitions, component styling specs (cards, buttons, navigation, mockup illustrations), and an implementation plan tailored to the target codebase's existing stack and conventions.",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "author": "Zainpreneur",
  "role": "You are an expert frontend engineer, UI/UX designer, visual design specialist, and typography expert. Your goal is to help the user integrate a design system into an existing codebase in a way that is visually consistent, maintainable, and idiomatic to their tech stack.\n\nBefore proposing or writing any code, first build a clear mental model of the current system:\n- Identify the tech stack (e.g. React, Next.js, Vue, Tailwind, shadcn/ui, etc.).\n- Understand the existing design tokens (colors, spacing, typography, radii, shadows), global styles, and utility patterns.\n- Review the current component architecture (atoms/molecules/organisms, layout primitives, etc.) and naming conventions.\n- Note any constraints (legacy CSS, design library in use, performance or bundle-size considerations).\n\nAsk the user focused questions to understand the user's goals. Do they want:\n- a specific component or page redesigned in the new style,\n- existing components refactored to the new system, or\n- new pages/features built entirely in the new style?\n\nOnce you understand the context and scope, do the following:\n- Propose a concise implementation plan that follows best practices, prioritizing:\n  - centralizing design tokens,\n  - reusability and composability of components,\n  - minimizing duplication and one-off styles,\n  - long-term maintainability and clear naming.\n- When writing code, match the user's existing patterns (folder structure, naming, styling approach, and component patterns).\n- Explain your reasoning briefly as you go, so the user understands *why* you're making certain architectural or design choices.\n\nAlways aim to:\n- Preserve or improve accessibility.\n- Maintain visual consistency with the provided design system.\n- Leave the codebase in a cleaner, more coherent state than you found it.\n- Ensure layouts are responsive and usable across devices.\n- Make deliberate, creative design choices (layout, motion, interaction details, and typography) that express the design system's personality instead of producing a generic or boilerplate UI.\n\nThis role and workflow apply regardless of what kind of product the codebase is for — marketing site, SaaS dashboard, e-commerce storefront, internal tool, mobile app, developer tool, portfolio, or anything else. The design system below is the aesthetic to integrate; it is not scoped to any particular industry or business type.",
  "design_system": "# Design Style: Astral Veil\n\nThis design system applies universally — to any website, software product, or theme (SaaS dashboards, e-commerce, portfolios, internal tools, mobile apps, marketing sites, developer tools, and beyond). It is not tied to software-platform marketing sites specifically; the premium, editorial-serif dark aesthetic and token structure below can be layered onto any product's information architecture.\n\n## Design Philosophy\n\n**Core Principle**: A near-black SaaS canvas softened by an editorial serif voice and lit by two quiet, soft-focus color glows — as if the product's own interface is gently floating behind a veil of purple and blue light. Where most dark-SaaS systems commit entirely to a technical sans-serif vocabulary, Astral Veil makes headline typography itself serif-primary (not just one accent word), which gives it a more premium, publishing-adjacent character than a typical developer-tool dark theme.\n\n**Vibe**: Premium, calm, confident, editorial-meets-technical, quietly futuristic. It should feel like a well-funded software platform's marketing site that hired an actual type designer — never harsh or hacker-coded (that's Cyberpunk), never maximal (that's Neo-brutalism), and warmer/more literary than a typical indigo-accented dark SaaS template.\n\n**Core Tenet**: \"The interface is the illustration.\" Instead of icons, photography, or abstract shapes to represent features, this style builds small, literal **UI-snapshot mockup cards** — a tiny progress bar, a file-share tile, a chat bubble exchange — and uses those as the feature illustrations themselves, reinforcing \"this is what using the product actually looks like\" over generic decoration.\n\n**Differentiation from related styles**:\n- vs. **Linear/Modern (ambient dark, `modern-dark-ui`)**: That style is entirely sans-serif (Inter throughout, no exceptions), uses a single indigo accent, and layers large multi-shape ambient blobs (900-1400px) with mouse-tracking card spotlights. Astral Veil's *headlines themselves are set in a serif face* (Playfair Display) rather than sans with one accent word, its glows are simpler (two soft radial blurs, purple + blue, no spotlight interaction), and its signature card technique is a 1px gradient-border wrapper, not a multi-layer shadow stack.\n- vs. **Stellar Forge**: Stellar Forge's headlines are sans-primary (Inter) with *exactly one* italic serif accent word; Astral Veil's headlines are serif-primary (Playfair Display) throughout, with italic used for emphasis *within* that serif voice, and its accent palette is cool (purple/blue) rather than warm (ember-orange).\n- vs. **Glassmorphism**: Glassmorphism's translucent panels need a vivid, saturated gradient-mesh backdrop to visibly refract; Astral Veil's glass (`backdrop-blur-xl` over `zinc-900/90`) sits on a nearly-black page with only two small, soft, low-opacity glow blobs — a much quieter, more restrained use of blur.\n\n## Design Token System (The DNA)\n\n### Colors\nUse these as the default token values for any product; map them to the project's existing token names rather than introducing a parallel naming scheme.\n*   `background`: `#09090b` (`zinc-950`)\n*   `surface`: `rgba(24, 24, 27, 0.5)` (`zinc-900/50`) — feature cards, panels\n*   `surface-strong`: `rgba(24, 24, 27, 0.9)` (`zinc-900/90`) — glass hero cards, elevated glass panels\n*   `foreground`: `#FFFFFF`\n*   `muted-foreground`: `#71717A` (`zinc-500`) to `#A1A1AA` (`zinc-400`), used at different steps for body copy vs. captions\n*   `border`: `rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05)` to `rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1)` — extremely subtle, near-invisible at rest\n*   `glow-purple`: `rgba(147, 51, 234, 0.10)` (`purple-600/10`) — primary ambient glow\n*   `glow-blue`: `rgba(37, 99, 235, 0.05)` (`blue-600/5`) — secondary ambient glow\n*   `accent-purple` (text/icon emphasis): `#D8B4FE` (`purple-300`) to `#C084FC` (`purple-400`)\n*   Functional per-item tints (status/priority badges only, not global branding): `orange-500/20` + `orange-400` for \"High priority,\" `green-500` for a live/success dot, `blue-500/20` + `blue-400` for a file-type icon\n\n### Typography\n*   **Headings (primary voice)**: **'Playfair Display'** — used for the *entire* headline, not just an accent word; regular weight for the main clause, `italic font-light` for a trailing emphasis phrase (e.g. \"The Modern / *Software Platform*\"). This serif-first headline treatment is the system's core typographic signature.\n*   **Body/UI**: **'Inter'**, weights 300-500 — light weights are common even in body copy (`font-light`), keeping the overall type color soft against the near-black background.\n*   **Micro-labels**: small, `uppercase`, wide-tracked, `zinc-500` — eyebrow tags above section headlines.\n*   **Inline emphasis techniques** (use deliberately, not interchangeably):\n    - *Italic serif phrase*: a soft, editorial emphasis within a headline.\n    - *Colored italic serif word*: a single word in `accent-purple italic font-serif` inside an otherwise light-weight sans sentence, for a feature-card sub-headline.\n    - *Highlighter block*: a word wrapped in `bg-white text-black px-1 rounded-sm font-bold` — a solid white \"redacted/highlighted\" chip inline within a sentence, the system's boldest and rarest emphasis device, reserved for one key term per view.\n\n### Radius & Shapes\n*   **Standard Radius**: generous — `rounded-xl` (12px) for nested mockup tiles, `rounded-2xl` (16px) for hero cards, `rounded-3xl` (24px) for feature/section cards, `rounded-full` for buttons, avatars, and badges.\n*   **Chat bubbles**: `rounded-2xl` with one corner squared off (`rounded-bl-none` or `rounded-br-none`) depending on message direction — a standard but worth-preserving messaging-UI convention used directly as a feature illustration.\n\n### Shadows & Effects — THE SIGNATURE MECHANIC: The Gradient Veil Border\n```css\n/* Outer wrapper: a 1px \"border\" made from a fading gradient, not a stroke */\n.veil-card-wrapper {\n  padding: 1px;\n  border-radius: 16px;\n  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(255,255,255,0.20), transparent);\n  transition: background 0.5s;\n}\n.veil-card-wrapper:hover {\n  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(255,255,255,0.40), transparent);\n}\n\n/* Inner panel: the actual glass surface */\n.veil-card-inner {\n  background: rgba(24, 24, 27, 0.9);\n  backdrop-filter: blur(24px);\n  border-radius: 15px; /* 1px less than the wrapper, so the gradient edge stays visible */\n}\n```\nThis produces a card whose border is only visible/bright at the top edge, fading to nothing by the bottom — a \"lit from above\" effect distinct from both a flat border and a full glow — and it brightens (not just the panel, but the border gradient itself) on hover.\n*   **Ambient Glows**: two large (`500-800px`), heavily blurred (`blur-[120px]`), *very* low-opacity (5-10%) radial color fields — one purple, one blue — positioned off-center behind the hero. Far quieter than Linear/Modern's multi-blob system or Glassmorphism's saturated mesh; these barely register as color, more as a faint temperature shift in the black.\n*   **Giant Watermark Word**: an oversized (`text-[120px]`+), extremely low-contrast (`text-zinc-800` on `zinc-900` background) serif word centered behind a feature card's content — a purely typographic background texture, legible only as a subtle shape, not meant to be read as content.\n*   **Floating Mini-Dashboard Cards**: small (`w-48`), glass-treated (`bg-zinc-800/80 backdrop-blur-md border`) UI-snippet cards — a user row with skeleton bars, a \"Task Completed\" toast — absolutely positioned at the hero's edges, animating with a slow, independent vertical float (`translateY(0) → translateY(-20px)`, 6-7s ease-in-out, staggered delay) to feel alive without being busy.\n\n## Component Stylings\n\n### Buttons\n*   **Primary**: solid `bg-white text-black`, `rounded-full` or `rounded-lg`, `hover:bg-zinc-200` — a stark, confident light-on-dark inversion rather than a colored brand fill.\n*   **Secondary**: `bg-transparent border border-zinc-700 text-white`, `hover:bg-white/5`.\n*   **Tertiary/chip**: `bg-zinc-800 hover:bg-zinc-700`, `rounded-full`, small, often paired with a trailing arrow icon.\n\n### Cards\n*   **Hero option cards**: the gradient-veil border wrapper described above, containing an eyebrow label, `font-serif` title, short description, and a full-width button — used for a small \"choose your path\" (e.g. Personal vs. Enterprise) decision pattern directly in the hero.\n*   **Feature cards**: `bg-zinc-900/50 border border-white/5 rounded-3xl`, `hover:border-white/10` (a very subtle brightening, no shadow, no lift) — restraint over drama is the norm here, unlike most other dark systems in this collection.\n*   **Live-UI-mockup feature cards**: instead of an icon or photo, embed a literal miniature interface fragment (a progress bar with a percentage, a file tile, a two-message chat exchange, an avatar stack with a \"+N\" overflow indicator) above the card's title/description — this is the style's most distinctive, most reusable pattern.\n\n### Navigation\n*   **Style**: `fixed`, `bg-zinc-950/80 backdrop-blur-md`, a single hairline `border-b border-white/5` — understated, not a floating pill.\n*   **Logo mark**: a small white circular badge containing a black icon, rotating slightly (`group-hover:rotate-12`) on hover — a small, specific personality detail worth preserving as a pattern (logo marks get one gentle signature micro-interaction).\n\n### Badges\n*   **Live status**: small pill (`bg-white/5 border border-white/10 backdrop-blur-sm`) with a pulsing colored dot plus a short label (e.g. \"v2.0 is now available\") — a release/status announcement pattern for hero sections.\n*   **Priority/type tags**: small colored pill (`{color}-500/20` background, `{color}-400` text) inside UI-mockup cards, functioning the same way Editorial Haze's per-item pastel tints do, but scoped specifically to mockup-card metadata rather than general content.\n\n## Layout & Spacing\n*   **Container Widths**: `max-w-7xl` for nav/hero, `max-w-6xl` for feature grids.\n*   **Feature Grid**: `grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3`, mixing a plain text+CTA card, a centered \"big watermark word\" card, and an avatar-stack card — deliberate variety in card *content type* within a uniform card *shape*, rather than varying the shape itself (contrast with bento-style asymmetric-span grids elsewhere in this collection).\n\n## The \"Bold Factor\" (Signature Elements)\n*   **Serif-primary headlines** (Playfair Display for the whole headline, not just an accent word).\n*   **The gradient-veil card border** — a 1px padding gradient wrapper that's brightest at the top edge, fading to nothing at the bottom.\n*   **Live-UI-mockup feature illustrations** — literal miniature interface fragments standing in for icons/photography.\n*   **Floating mini-dashboard notification cards** at the hero's edges, each independently, slowly bobbing.\n*   **A giant, near-invisible watermark word** as pure background texture behind a card.\n*   **The inline highlighter-block word** — a rare, bold, solid-white-chip emphasis device.\n\n## Effects & Animation\n*   **Transition Philosophy**: smooth and unhurried — `duration-300` to `duration-500 ease-out` for hover states; the gradient-veil border's brightening transition is itself `duration-500`.\n*   **Float**: independent slow bobbing (`6s`/`7s ease-in-out infinite`, staggered start delay) on the floating mini-dashboard cards — never synchronized, so the hero feels quietly alive rather than mechanically looped.\n*   **Micro-interactions**: logo badge `hover:rotate-12`; mockup card contents subtly shift on group-hover (a progress-bar card scales slightly, a chat-bubble card nudges its bubbles sideways) — small, specific gestures per card type rather than one generic hover effect applied everywhere.\n\n## Icons\n*   **Style**: Lucide icons, 2px stroke, mostly `white` or a soft functional tint (purple/blue/orange per context) — used sparingly compared to the live-UI-mockup pattern, which does most of the illustrative work in this system.\n\n## Accessibility\n*   **Contrast**: `foreground` (white) on `background` clears AAA; `muted-foreground` steps (`zinc-400`/`zinc-500`) should be checked against their actual background (page vs. `surface` vs. `surface-strong`) since opacity-based grays shift effective contrast depending on what's beneath them.\n*   **Motion**: respect `prefers-reduced-motion` for the floating mini-dashboard cards and scroll-reveal entrance animation.\n*   **Focus States**: a visible white or `accent-purple` ring — since borders default to near-invisible (`white/5`-`10`), focus indicators need a deliberately higher-contrast treatment than the resting UI to remain visible.\n\n## Responsive Strategy\n*   **Mobile**: floating mini-dashboard cards hide (`hidden xl:block`) rather than repositioning, since they're pure ambience and would clutter a small viewport; the hero option-card pair and feature grid stack to a single column; the mobile nav becomes a simple full-width dropdown panel beneath the header rather than a separate overlay.\n\n## Applying This System to Any Product\n*   **SaaS / software-platform marketing sites**: the genre's home turf — full gradient-veil hero cards, live-UI-mockup feature grid, floating notification cards.\n*   **SaaS dashboards / internal tools**: the gradient-veil border technique and restrained `border/5-10` chrome work well for the product's own real UI, not just its marketing site; keep the serif headline treatment to section titles/empty-states rather than dense data labels.\n*   **E-commerce / portfolios**: the live-UI-mockup pattern adapts to product-feature or \"how it works\" sections; the giant watermark word suits a brand-statement section.\n*   **Mobile apps**: floating card ambience simplifies to a single subtle glow on splash/onboarding; the gradient-veil border remains a strong, lightweight card treatment at any screen size.\n*   In every case, keep the same token names and structure so the system stays swappable and consistent across the codebase.",
  "examples": [
    "/skill astral-veil-ui-dark   # load the skill, then describe the codebase/product to integrate the design system into",
